# The C Standard is Pretty Old

- First "Informal" Specification: "The C Programming Language"
  Book/Manual, Published 1978  
  by Brian **K**ernighan and Dennis **R**itchie (K&R C)

- First Formal Standard: ANSI X3.159-1989  
  by the X3J11 Committee  
  of the American National Standards Institution (ANSI)

- First Formal **International** Standard: ISO/IEC 9899:1990

![ISO/IEC Logo](source/img/iso-logo64.png)



## C23 (formally ISO/IEC 9899:2024) released

C2y (likely C29) in progress from Working Group 14 (WG14)

![A screenshot from the ISO website showing the latest C published C standard, ISO/IEC 9899 - 2024, available for purchase](source/img/iso9899-purchase.png)



## Nice!

Which brings us to two extremely similar questions:

- "Should I Update The Standard of My Language?" (e.g. C, C++, Fortran, JS, ...)
- "Should I Standardize™ My Language?" (Zig, Rust, ...)



## The Answer is the Same for Both of These

And that answer is...



## It Depends!

We can answer it by looking at what Standardization has done  
for us, **MATERIALLY**, in these 3 cases for the ISO C Standard:

- `typeof`, introduced in C23
- `<threads.h>`'s thread features, introduced in C11
- `realloc` Undefined Behavior, introduced in C23
